### Page 3 of 41

```text
September 15, 2014
Letter from the Director
1
Letter from the Director
September 15, 2014
Our Mayor and City Council address issues regarding growth in Cheyenne on a daily
basis.  With this growth there has been an increased demand for public services - including
cemetery services.  Our cemeteries are quickly reaching their capacity and it is my opinion
that appropriate consideration should be given to the necessary expansion. This report
and its supporting documentation validate the need for expanding the cemetery system to
continue providing the public service that residents have relied on for 142 years.
Supported by sound economic analysis and industry research, the expansion of the
municipal cemetery system is a pro-active endeavor to insure cemetery services to the
community well into the next century.  The need to seek additional property for expansion
was first identified in internal reviews performed in 1989 and 2007.  Today, cemetery staff
recognize the failing inventory of available cemetery spaces and understand the need to
seek expansion as quickly as possible.  Design, development and implementation of a new
cemetery will require the City to move wisely and quickly to allow ample time for a transition
without an interruption of the services currently offered.
The public relies on the certainty that is afforded them by the stability of a municipal cemetery
system.  It is an inarguable fact that the citizens of Cheyenne enjoy certain security with
the knowledge that their city will not abandon a cemetery that is owned and operated by
their city government.  It is expected, because of the historic operation of the municipal
cemetery system, that future services will be available, even when existing facilities are full.
Jason Sanchez
Director of Grounds & Facilities
City of Cheyenne
“Show me the manner in which a community cares for its dead and
I will measure with mathematical exactness the tender mercies of
its people, their respect for the laws of the land and their loyalty to
high ideals.”
                                                   ~ Sir William Gladstone
