```text
PRIMARY GOALS
• Develop the site in consideration of its future potential as a destination for experiencing Wyoming’s
regional heritage and history so as not to preclude incorporation into a future regional park.
• Identify opportunites for shared use with multipurpose facilities that serve the cemetery and
adjacent public user groups while preserving the natural and cultural resources of the site.
• Maintain the open view qualities of a high plains landscape from critical viewpoints as identified
in the Cemetery Arboretum Master Plan and the High Plains Arboretum Master Plan.
• Circulation system should consider expansion beyond the 25 acre planning area and enhance
access to the adjacent High Plains Arboretum.
• Include interment and memorialization comparable with current market offerings such as:
o	 Committal Shelters
o	 Cenotaph Walls
o	 Ossuaries
o	 Ceremonial Plaza’s
o	 Columbaria
• Retain for interpretive and educational purposes significant historical features as identified in
both the High Plains Arboretum Master Plan and in the 2014 cultural landscape report conducted
by the Veteran’s Administration.
• Extend the arboretum quality plantings through the cemetery for pedestrian comfort and to
enhance aesthetic value of the cemetery.
SECONDARY GOALS
• Phase future development in ways that enhance sales of existing interment offerings and
preserve flexibility to respond and adjust to trends.
• Interment capacity should reflect a 50 year plan
• When expanding the cemetery beyond the 25 acre planning area, maintain the open park-like,
aesthetic quality of the cemetery by including group destinations within the landscape, especially
near trail crossings.  These destinations should include opportunities for memorialization that is
in line with current trends at the time of expansion.
ADJACENCY GOALS
• Major program elements such as committal shelter and columbaria should be sized to accomodate
ceremonial and event use
• Maintain access to existing facilities and research plots
VISITOR RELATED PROGRAM ELEMENTS
    CEMETERY ARBORETUM
• Centralized gateway and offices for visitor contact
o	 Adjacent short term parking
o	 Interment kiosk
o	 Office space for 2 staff members
o	 Restroom
• Committal Shelter
o	 Adjacent parking and access to offices
o	 Enclosable for protection from wind and weather
o	 Capacity for concurrent or overlapping committal schedules
• Maintenance Area
o	 Enclosed 40’x60’ maintenance building
o	 Exterior wash area
o	 Access to cemetery grounds
o	 Screened or separated from public view
o	 Fuel station/ storage
• Roads with on street parking capacity
• Formal parking for large events and adjacent overflow parking capacity
• Intuitive and formalized wayfinding
• Road alignment that allows for future site access points
    USDA
• Capacity for introducing a future public visitation component
    HIGH PLAINS ARBORETUM
• Pedestrian access to arboretum specimens within the cemetery
• Capacity to implement the original master plan without major re-design or loss of resources

    VETERAN’S ADMINISTRATION (National Burial Grounds)
• 5 acres of interment area to include 3 acre first phase
• Flag assembly area
These categories have been identified as having potential for shared use by adjacent stakeholders
September 15, 2014
Cemetery Goals and Program Elements
2
NARRATIVE
Located adjacent to the former horticultural research station and High Plains Arboretum, this new site will complement the exsiting municipal cemeteries and provide long term
expansion resources for the region. Synergy between the USDA, the High Plains Arboretum, the Veteran’s Administration and the City of Cheyenne elevate the value of landscape
within which the cemetery will reside.  With the Cheyenne Cemetery Arboretum the families of Cheyenne will have a respectful and appropriate final resting place for its people.
A cemetery stakeholder advisory team, established by the City of Cheyenne, included stakeholders from adjacent user groups as well as city and county staff.  This stakeholder
group, led by the consultant firm of Robert Peccia and Associates, developed a refined set of project goals and program elements as well as a heierarchy for their implementation.
