September 15, 2014
Background and Context for the Master Plan
3
PURPOSE OF THE MASTER PLAN
The expansion of the municipal cemetery system is a pro-active endeavor to insure cemetery services
for the community well into the next century. An internal status review of the cemetery system, performed
in both 1989 and 2005, indicated a need to seek additional property to continue cemetery services. It is
estimated that the current facilities will be at capacity* in approximately 5 years.
The City of Cheyenne has provided these services for over 130 years. City of Cheyenne, Parks &
Recreation Department, Cemetery Division staff recognizes the failing inventory of available cemetery
spaces and understands the need to seek expansion as quickly as possible. Land acquisition, design,
development and implementation of a new cemetery will require the City to move wisely and quickly to
allow ample time for this transition.
Recently, the City of Cheyenne Parks & Recreation Department, Cemetery Division completed an analysis of
options for a future cemetery expansion. (INCLUDE IN APPENDIX – Analysis of Municipal Cemetery Options
by JST-Architects & L.F. Sloane Consulting) This analysis recommended that the City of Cheyenne should
continue with a public cemetery program, that the preferred site is adjacent to the High Plains Arboretum and
that an expansion should be implemented as soon as practical.
The City of Cheyenne has also received a recent inquiry from the Department of Veteran’s Affairs Administration
concerning the development of a rural Veteran’s cemetery in the vicinity of the High Plain Arboretum.  The
co-location of a designated Veteran’s cemetery and a city cemetery would be advantageous to regional
residents.
