September 15, 2014
Background and Context for the Master Plan
4
CEMETERY MASTER PLANNING OBJECTIVES
The initial objectives of the project as deﬁ ned by the City of Cheyenne were to:
Evaluate various schematic design options for an arboretum style cemetery located at the High Plains Arboretum
in Cheyenne, Wyoming.
Develop a master plan for the project area to provide a path to move forward with the expansion of the City of
Cheyenne’s municipal cemetery system.
Evaluate the city’s current operating plan and fee structure and to provide a sound operating/business plan for the
greatest amount of solvency for the municipal cemetery expansion.
Integrate adjacent stakeholder interests and integrate previous master planning efforts of the High Plains Arboretum
Allow for expansion over time
MASTER PLAN PROCESS
After the analysis and recommendations for a municipal cemetery expansion were made by JST Architects & LF Sloane
Consulting, the City of Cheyenne solicited for planning and design services through a public qualiﬁ cations and proposal
process.   The Robert Peccia and Associates (RPA) team was selected to complete the master planning of the 25 acre
site to include an economic analysis of the operating plan and fee structure as compared to local and regional cemeteries.
Lead team members were already familiar with the site’s history having authored the High Plains Arboretum Master Plan
adjacent to the proposed project site.
Planning work began in November of 2013 with a series of three stakeholder workshops to conﬁ rm and elaborate on the
planning objectives and to collaborate on establishing the criteria for making master planning decisions for the project.
Site analysis and context maps were developed to assist the team in understanding the site’s physical, climatological,
land use, infrastructure and cultural inﬂ uences.  These are summarized in Figures 3 - 6.
Each workshop built on planning decisions made and input gathered from prior work sessions.  Intermediate work sessions
were also held with a core planning group and an expanded team from the Veteran’s Administration to discuss site speciﬁ c
opportunities for shared use facilities at the site.
MAJOR STAKEHOLDERS
In order to allow for an informed decision making process several stakeholders interests were reviewed at the ﬁ rst
workshop.  Prior master planning efforts on the project site by the High Plains Arboretum as well as current operations at
the USDA and ongoing Veteran’s Administration site selection efforts were reviewed.
Justin Derner of the Cheyenne based USDA facility provided input on current land use and operations within and adjacent
to the USDA project site. The Veteran’s Commision Director Larry Bartelbort represented the interests of the proposed
National Burial Grounds within the City of Cheyenne.  Director Shane Smith and members from the Friends Board of
Directors represented the High Plains Arboretum.  Two of the principal consultants from the RPA team were the authors
of the 2003 HPA master plan and brought a working knowledge of the site’s opportunities and constraints.
IDENTIFYING FUTURE STEPS
Through the process of developing the Cemetery Arboretum Master Plan, several contextual elements of the site suggested
the need for identifying and preserving and enhancing other resources of the 2000 acre city owned land.  This includes
but is not limited to the portion of the city owned property that is currently part of a long term lease to the USDA.  Future
planning for the preservation and enhancement of the site could secure these lands as a future Regional Park and open
space for the residents of Cheyenne.
