SUMMARY OF INFLUENCES OF ADJACENT STAKEHOLDERS
The USDA is the principal land user in the area operating under a long term lease agreement with the
City of Cheyenne.  The city owned property has been utilized for agricultural research by the USDA
on a majority of the 2000 acres outlined in Figure 1.  This includes reasearch, ofﬁ ce and maintenance
facilities on the core campus north and west of the USDA Meadow.  Continued access to agricultural
trials from Hildreth Road and the un-named gravel road west of the property were critical to continued
operations at the facility.  As an extention of the Fort Collins USDA, the Cheyenne USDA station is part
of the Presidential Climate Hub designation. Public access to the facility may increase general site
visitation in the future.  While the magnitude of this future USDA component is to be developed further,
a consideration of common site access, visitor contact and parking near the southern portion of the core
campus were integrated throughout the planning process.
In addition to the USDA facilities, there are remnants of previous research on the former horticultural
research station including a lath house and greenhouse and surviving woody plants and windbreaks.  A
majority of these features are north of the line shown in ﬁ gure 4.  For planning purposes, development
of interment areas will focus on property south of this line pending a concurrent cultural resources study
conducted by the Department of Veteran’s affairs.
The previous preservation efforts and planning for a High Plains Arboretum (HPA) in 2003 were reviewed
and summarized for integration into the 2013-2014 Cemetery Arboretum Master planning objectives
(ﬁ gure 5).  It was important to the stakeholder team that the cemetery planning objectives and solutions
be developed so as not to preclude the acheivement of the original goals and objectives of the High
Plains Arboretum Master Plan.  These were to preserve, protect and enhance the assets of the former
research station located on the property.  The full High Plains Arboretum Master Plan is included in the
Appendix.
The priority zones identiﬁ ed below were developed during the workshop in order to establish criteria for
introducing road access to the future cemetery through the HPA.  The future HPA visitor center, demonstration
pond and gardens of the master plan were to be preserved while the 2003 planned parking conﬁ guration and
location were made available for cemetery site access or redevelopment with the cemetery planning efforts.
Prior to the start of the Cheyenne Cemetery Arboretum Master Plan, the Veteran’s Administration had already
begun the process of reviewing available lands for a 5 acre National Burial Grounds to serve the Cheyenne
and regional community.  This included consideration of an existing meadow north of Hildreth Road adjacent
to the ofﬁ ce and research buildings of the USDA campus.  Offsite locations considered by the VA were not
disclosed to the planning team, however, a location within the project work limits was identiﬁ ed through the
planning process as a preferred option that could integrate more cohesively with the municipal cemetery
without interrupting the existing and planned improvements of the High Plains Arboretum.
Through a series of workshops the following elements were identiﬁ ed as potential shared use and beneﬁ cial
to all stakeholders if developed in harmony with the cemetery arboretum:
The HPA event meadow in Zone 2 with an integrated cemetery event use area.
Shared parking if located to still serve the needs of the High Plains Arboretum and future USDA visitor
contact center in the former USDA lab building.
Road conﬁ gurations located near the southern HPA perimeter as it would improve site access and
promote shared public use of both facilities.  Arboretum trials are currently only accessed by foot.
The historic remaining demonstration shelterbelt of the HPA has a short term value for educational
purposes but is not a part of the current HPA lease agreement.  Improved access to this portion of the
property through the cemetery arboretum planning process would be beneﬁ cial but not critical to the
planning efforts of the High Plains Arboretum.  A future shelterbelt within the HPA lease boundary will
be established per the long term objectives of the 2003 master plan.
