7
SUMMARY OF SPATIAL ORGANIZATION AND SITE ACCESS
Several organizational and circulation options were considered and vetted with stakeholders in the
planning process (ﬁ gure 7 and appendix). The existing site approach along Roundtop and Hildreth
Roads provides immediate paved access to the Cemetery, the USDA and the High Plains Arboretum.
From a short term planning perspective this planning approach requires minimal investment in providing
site access for all of the site users (Option 3A).
In consideration of long term planning for future phases of a municipal cemetery expansion, an alternate
main entry has been integrated with this planning effort (Option 3B).  The approach would occur along
Experimental Farm Road to the south of the city property and access the site from a currently unimproved
road on the western property boundary.  For both entry approaches, consideration of future secondary
entry points into the site would improve user access and circulation to an expanded arboretum cemetery.
Introduction of the Experimental Farm Road entry has the added beneﬁ t of separation of cemetery
visitors and High Plains Arboretum visitors.
While the city owned future expansion lands are currently in use under a long term lease by the USDA,
consideration of future access points for a cemetery expansion to the south is allowed for in the planning
process.  There is the potential for 390 acres of future cemetery expansion within the conﬁ nes of the
existing perimeter road network south of the phase 1 limits on city owned land.
Developing a 5 acre footprint for the National Burial Grounds within the cemetery new project limits
allowed for the sharing of facilities and infrastructure that might not have been considered independantly
for cost reasons.  These included a shared committal shelter and contact ofﬁ ce for receiving visitors
to both cemeteries.  Adaptive re-use of the existing greenhouse research ofﬁ ce as a main contact center or
gateway for all visitors led to the acceptance of the preferred relationship option shown with an allowance for
future site access along the currently undeveloped portion of Experimental Farm Road.
This sound economic development option provides maximum overlap of shared infrastucture and facility
development and does not preclude the future plans for an expanded High Plains Arboretum.  Other shared
cost beneﬁ ts would include a pumphouse, pumping equipment, raw water line and maintenance facilities.
Experimental Farm Road
Roundtop Road
