### Page 14 of 41

```text
NARRATIVE:
The entry approach experience along Hildreth Road
introduces the regional qualities of the landscape and
provides sweeping views of the arboretum plantings
and interpretive trails.  Visitors arrive at a cemetery
gateway and visitor contact center and have the option
of stopping at the wayﬁ nding kiosk or visiting the
cemetery ofﬁ ce for an orientation.  Close in parking is
available at the ofﬁ ces and the road network allows for
dispersed parking along all interment area roads.
Arboretum windbreak shelterbelts that parallel an
informal meandering road and trail network is proposed
for maximum pedestrian comfort and wind protection.
These sheltered serenity trails are extensions of the
interpretive trails of the High Plains Arboretum and offer
strolling opportunities through the cemetery arboretum.
Integrated with the serenity trails are modest planted
berms integrated with memorial concepts that will
include cenotaph walls, columbaria and ossuaries.
Mausoleum style interments are located to the south of
each of the serenity trails.
The High Plains Arboretum is responsible for
maintaining the shelter belts and trails and for providing
recommendations of plantings that both support the
history of the regionally signiﬁ cant site and that serve
the role of a learning landscape.
BIRDSEYE VIEWPOINTS:
1. Entry Gateway, Committal Shelters and Cemetery
Arboretum Facilities
2. Interment Organization, Shelterbelt Trail and
National Burial Grounds Entry
3. Mausoleum Row, Loop Roads, Monumented
Intersections and Dispersed Parking
4. Columbarium Plaza and High Plains Arboretum
Interface
5. Meadow Viewshed Looking Southwest
