September 15, 2014
ECONOMIC ANALYSIS
Operating Plan and Fee Structure
Cheyenne Municipal Cemetery Expansion Master Plan – Cheyenne, Wyoming
May 1, 2014
EvaluaƟ on of Current OperaƟ ng Plan and Fee Structure
Research objecƟ ves for the Cheyenne Cemetery Arboretum Master Plan, were to establish a baseline for comparison
of levels of service and how services are provided within the project area and to review current operaƟ ons at the City.
Data will aid to validate or reconﬁ gure elements of the Master Plan and aid in idenƟ fying future opportuniƟ es at the new
cemetery.
PROJECTED CAPACITY
The current number of desirable, conƟ guous, non-military spaces available in Lakeview and Beth El cemeteries is esƟ mated
at 399. Based on demand calculaƟ on of between 60 and 100 burial spaces sold annually, the exisƟ ng City of Cheyenne
municipal cemetery has between 4 to 6 years unƟ l the desirable spaces/plots are sold out. This assumes that burials
conƟ nue on a status quo burial path with approximately one-third cremaƟ ons to two-thirds tradiƟ onal burials. Growth in
demand will be dependent on populaƟ on growth and mortality rates in Cheyenne.
MAJOR TRENDS AND STATISTICS
The parameters for operaƟ ons and maintenance analysis for the Cemetery Arboretum Master Plan included an internet and
telephone survey of three cemeteries with similar characterisƟ cs and demographics to the Cheyenne market area. Those
cemeteries included Greenhill Cemetery in Laramie, Wyoming (Albany County), Highland cemetery in Casper, Wyoming
(Natrona County), and Fairview cemetery in ScoƩ sbluﬀ , NE (ScoƩ sbluﬀ  County). InformaƟ on on three addiƟ onal Colorado
cemeteries  was included in the comparison table using data from a report that was completed a year ago. The informaƟ on
collected from these cemeteries included fees, services, interments, space available, expansion plans and various trends in
burials in the various locales. This informaƟ on gives a snapshot of how the City of Cheyenne is performing in comparison
to other cemeteries and will contribute to recommendaƟ ons on future fee structures and expected trends at the new site.
In order to understand local cemetery facility and interment trends, addiƟ onal background research is included from
documents found via the Internet on a naƟ onal level to provide a basis for the quesƟ ons presented to the operators
at the other cemeteries. A brief study of naƟ onal trends and staƟ sƟ cs was conducted in order to understand naƟ onal
trends regarding forecasted deaths and trends that might be implemented at the new Cemetery Arboretum. A number of
research arƟ cles and previous cemetery master plans, market studies, and ﬁ nancial plans were reviewed and studied in
order to extract relevant informaƟ on.
The informaƟ on gathered for this review is broken into naƟ onal trends and regional trends, both of which form the basis
of the Local Cemeteries Comparison Study developed from data collected in the telephone quesƟ onnaire.
NATIONAL TRENDS
Deaths and PopulaƟ on Base
Main ﬁ ndings of a research project on U.S. PopulaƟ on ProjecƟ ons: 2012 to 2060 completed by the US. Dept. of Commerce,
Economics and StaƟ sƟ cs AdministraƟ on, U.S. Census Bureau shows that populaƟ on is projected to grow more slowly,
conƟ nue aging and become more diverse.
“The Census Bureau has made adjustments to the third major underlying assumpƟ on in its populaƟ on projecƟ ons, the
number of deaths. In general, life expectancy has been increasing. From 2012 to 2050, the projecƟ ons assume a total 128.4
million deaths, compared with 133.1 million deaths by 2050 that were assumed in the 2008 projecƟ ons, a slight diﬀ erence.
Another minor factor in lowering the long-term projecƟ ons was that the bureau used as its starƟ ng point a slightly lower
populaƟ on esƟ mate for 2011 than had been projected in 2008. The starƟ ng point for the new projecƟ ons was a U.S.
populaƟ on of 311.6 million, which is 1.6 million lower than the bureau had projected in 2008.
In a posƟ ng on the bureau’s Random Samplings blog, the bureau’s Jennifer M. Ortman explained that the agency has tried to “...
Strike a balance between recent trends and longer term trends in making assumpƟ ons about immigraƟ on and births.” For death
rates, she said, the bureau assumed that recent declines in old-age mortality will conƟ nue to some extent.”
Because of the increase in life expectancy the number of deaths is not anƟ cipated to be as dramaƟ c as would be expected because
of the aging baby boomers. Deaths between 2012 and 2060 are esƟ mated to grow from around 2.5 million in 2012 to 4.0 million
in 2060. In 2012, 14 percent of the US populaƟ on was 65 and older. In 2060 that populaƟ on will represent 22 percent of total
populaƟ on.
Types of Burials
Historically, typical burials involved in ground interment in a shroud or coﬃ  n and have developed to the complex full-body burial
tradiƟ on much of North America is familiar with today: embalmed remains in a manufactured casket, set in a concrete vault in a
municipality-owned or privately owned burial ground. As other interment opƟ ons have developed, modern cemeteries now oﬀ er
a complex variety of memorializaƟ on and burial choices. The naƟ onal trends research covered a wide variety of interment and
memorializaƟ on opƟ ons with one outstanding major trend: cremaƟ on.
According to the CremaƟ on AssociaƟ on of North America (CANA) the U.S. cremaƟ on rate reached
43 percent in 2012, and the total number of cremaƟ ons exceeded one million (Singhal LLC 2013).
Once a cremaƟ on choice is made, a mulƟ tude of opƟ ons are available for interment. These opƟ ons include but are not limited
to: cremains earth interment, columbarium niche, cremaƟ on garden, cremaƟ on estate, ossuary, cenotaph, memorial wall (with
remains kept by family or scaƩ ered), and scaƩ ering garden interment.
CANA predicts the cremaƟ on rate in the US will increase to 49 percent by 2016 and the 50 percent barrier broken in 2018. CANA’s
Annual StaƟ sƟ cs Report 2012 cited ﬁ ve major trends inﬂ uencing cremaƟ on rates:

Cost

Range of opƟ ons

Environmental impact

Geography

Religious acceptance
 “There’s no quesƟ on that cremaƟ ons are on the rise. Burials have been on the steady decline owing to economic hard Ɵ mes,
growing environmental consciousness, increased social acceptance, and a shiŌ  in religious values” (Singhal LLC 2013).
REGIONAL TRENDS
StaƟ sƟ cs and cited research reﬂ ect an increase in cremaƟ ons due to the following: social acceptance of cremaƟ ons, religious
acceptance of cremaƟ ons, environmental values, cost savings over full burial, and cost savings for transportaƟ on of the deceased
remains.
According to CANA the percentage of cremaƟ ons at death in Wyoming was 63% in 2012. StaƟ sƟ cally, in Wyoming, cremaƟ ons are
20 percent higher than the naƟ onal average (the naƟ onal average is 43 percent) and leading the naƟ onal trend. StaƟ sƟ cal data for
Wyoming’s burial trends is cited in CANA’s Annual StaƟ sƟ cs Report 2012 (Singhal LLC 2013). CremaƟ ons throughout the mountain
states are higher than most of the rest of the United States with most mountain states showing over 50 percent of burial trends
are cremaƟ ons.
