LOCAL TRENDS INTERVIEWS
In the absence of local staƟ sƟ cal data, one local funeral home and three cemeteries with somewhat comparable
demographics and geographical consideraƟ ons were queried for their insight into local cemetery needs. The opinions
provided conﬁ rm the naƟ onal and regional staƟ sƟ cal data and trending for the local cemetery interment market.
As menƟ oned above the three cemeteries interviewed included Greenhill Cemetery in Laramie, Wyoming (Albany County),
Highland cemetery in Casper, Wyoming (Natrona County), and Fairview cemetery in ScoƩ sbluﬀ , NE (ScoƩ sbluﬀ  County).
All three cemeteries concurred that cremaƟ ons have increased dramaƟ cally through the past 10 years and represent
between 40 percent and 60 percent of all burials in the three cemeteries surveyed (Leyba, Kelly, and Hunter, personal
communicaƟ on April 2014). Where interments used to be about 80 percent tradiƟ onal to 20 percent cremaƟ on, the raƟ o
is now about 50/50 and expected to conƟ nue to increase.
The City of Casper cemetery supervisor is also anƟ cipaƟ ng a reducƟ on in the number of interments of cremains since
many people are opƟ ng to hold on to the cremains or disperse them in other places besides the cemetery. This may have
the eﬀ ect of reducing the demand for addiƟ onal spaces in spite of the projected growth in burials from the baby boom
generaƟ on. In Laramie, the life of the cemetery has been extended to another 20 to 25 years due to the increase in the
number of cremaƟ ons. The city will also be acquiring 1 acre from the Catholic Church that is currently being maintained by
the city of Laramie and will provide an addiƟ onal 800 plots.
CURRENT CEMETERY OPERATIONS
Several planning documents have been prepared in the past several years including the Analysis of Municipal Cemetery
OpƟ ons for the City of Cheyenne completed in December, 2012 and the City of Cheyenne Cemetery Division Expansion
Project power point presentaƟ on which includes informaƟ on on current cemetery capacity. According to these documents
approximately 413 spaces are available in the Lakeview cemetery. Beth El cemetery has an esƟ mated 815 spaces available
of which 389 are for veteran’s burial and 426 for public burial. About one-third of the spaces are single, non-conƟ guous
spaces which are diﬃ  cult to sell. Therefore, the current number of desirable, conƟ guous, non-military spaces available in
Lakeview and Beth El are esƟ mated at 399.
The city cemetery has been in operaƟ on for 147 years. Each year an esƟ mated 220 to 250 burials occur in the city cemeteries. An
esƟ mated 60 to 100 burial spaces are sold annually, which suggests that the desirable lots could be sold out within 4 to 6 years.
Because of the increase in cremaƟ ons, which is expected to exceed 60 percent of all burials in the coming years based on regional
data, this build out could take longer since more cremains can be interred in one space than a tradiƟ onal burial.
The City of Cheyenne Parks and RecreaƟ on Department maintains 3 other cemeteries Mt Sinai (Jewish), Mt Olivet (Catholic), and
IOOF (Oddfellows). Since the City of Cheyenne provides maintenance to ﬁ ve cemeteries, city cemetery staﬃ  ng is 5 full Ɵ me and 1
quarter-Ɵ me plus seasonal workers.
There are 25,000 total interments in Lakeview and Beth El cemeteries. The city maintains all burial records for all cemeteries
maintained by the city. The City now has a computerized database system that is being upgraded which contains all records for all
the cemeteries.
The exisƟ ng city cemeteries (Lakeview and Beth El) have few spaces for sale; most of the spaces are on a fence line (less desirable),
or are single spaces with very few side-by side plots, and no available family plots. There is a demand and need for family plots. The
cemetery does not have cremaƟ on lots, but does have a columbarium (Beth El) and memorial wall (Lakeview). The angel statue is
a very popular spot for ceremonies at the memorial wall and could be incorporated into the new cemetery.
Speciﬁ c requests that are not available at some areas of the cemetery include upright monuments and dedicated plots (in the
ground) for cremaƟ ons.  Each cemetery space allows 3 interments (2 tradiƟ onal and 1 cremaƟ on, 1 tradiƟ onal and 2 cremaƟ ons,
or 3 cremaƟ ons). There is no cremaƟ on garden, scaƩ er garden, ossuary, or cenotaph. Green burials are regulated by the health
department which has green burial standards. Green burial has no embalming, only wood or bamboo caskets, or a shroud and no
headstone.
Community events at the cemetery include:

History walk – once per year

Candle vigil – once per year

Community service day (paint fences, collect leaves etc.) – several Ɵ mes per year

Element Church donates Ɵ me for community service in the cemetery as well as other city faciliƟ es every year
The Olivet (Catholic cemetery) has similar burial fees as the other municipal cemeteries. The city collects fees for all services at Mt
Olivet including maintenance, tent set-up and opening and closing sites plus an administraƟ ve and maintenance fee of $400 per
month. The City maintains two other cemeteries: Mt Sinai (Jewish) and IOOF (Oddfellows) cemeteries. There are no public burials
at either of these two cemeteries. The Mt Sinai cemetery was a land swap with the city. The city sells the spaces and maintains the
grounds, but only collects a fee for opening and closing the plots for Mt Sinai and IOOF. These private cemeteries pay fees on an
hourly basis. Salaries for sub-contracted work at the private cemeteries go into the General Fund. Any parts and supplies billed to
other cemeteries for sub-contracted services go directly to the city cemetery budget.
As shown in Table X.1 the city fees are slightly lower than Memorial Gardens cemetery and higher than Mountain View cemetery
in Cheyenne.  According to the cemetery superintendent, an immediate demand for 200 spaces including family plots exists in the
area for pre-sales and burials.
Current revenue generaƟ on includes space sales, opening and closing spaces, tent set-up, maintenance of other cemeteries,
monument locates and chapel rental. Some capacity for monumentaƟ on and engraving is available within the municipal cemetery
services oﬀ ered. This acƟ vity is likely to generate substanƟ al income to the cemetery operaƟ ons.
