September 15, 2014
All ﬁ ve cemeteries interviewed have adequate capacity for future interments. The esƟ mated annual interments range from
120 to 150 for 4 of the cemeteries surveyed; Mountain View Memorial Park has considerably fewer interments annually
at between 30 and 50 (Jacobi 2014). Since tradiƟ onal plots can handle from 1 to 8 interments per plot (at the cemeteries
surveyed) depending on the combinaƟ on of tradiƟ onal burials and cremains, space availability could be extended beyond
the anƟ cipated capacity.
Most of the cemeteries surveyed are in the process of adding alternaƟ ve burial interment opƟ ons, parƟ cularly a columbarium.
One supervisor menƟ oned a desire to have a chapel or commiƩ al shelter and another supervisor an ossuary. As discussed
above, many people are moving towards cremaƟ on and some have decided to retain the ashes, but may want a public
memorial for their deceased (memorial wall). Some people sƟ ll prefer to have their cremains buried in the ground rather
than in a niche in a columbarium. Many of the new interment opƟ ons are viable for the Cemetery Arboretum including
columbarium, memorial wall, ossuary, cenotaph, scaƩ ering gardens, green burial, etc. It appears that mausoleums are less
in demand and are not being incorporated in modern cemeteries as frequently as in the past.
Burial spaces, opening and closing and perpetual care fund fees vary among all the cemeteries.
As shown in Table X.1 the fees for burials for the City of Cheyenne are comparable or slightly higher than the fees in
Laramie, Casper, ScoƩ sbluﬀ , and Mountain View Memorial Park in Cheyenne ranging from $750 to $1,465 for an adult
tradiƟ onal burial. However, city fees are lower for a tradiƟ onal interment and comparable for cremains to Cheyenne
Memorial Gardens which provides more comparable services to the City of Cheyenne ($1,145 to $1,800 includes $600
vault). Vaults are required for adult interments at Memorial Gardens which increases the price of an adult burial. Cremains
burials range from $300 to $1,465. The City of Cheyenne shows the highest price for cremains burials. Infant burials are
substanƟ ally less expensive at Memorial Gardens and the other municipal cemeteries surveyed.
Comparison with the Colorado cemeteries shows that Cheyenne has a signiﬁ cantly lower adult burial fee (30% to 63% lower)
as compared to Loveland, Fort Collins, and Greeley cemeteries, but higher infant and cremaƟ on burial fees. Cheyenne also
has lower green burial fees ($1640 vs. $1925) compared to Fort Collins and similar to somewhat lower columbarium fees,
with fees ranging from $1,250 in Loveland to $1,965 in Greeley for a single columbarium. Cheyenne fee is $1,345.
All cemeteries oﬀ er addiƟ onal services at varying prices but generally are within a similar range to those charged by
Cheyenne municipal cemetery with the excepƟ on of disinterment where Cheyenne is considerably higher.
OperaƟ ons and maintenance budgets vary signiﬁ cantly between 4 of the cemeteries surveyed. Most municipal cemeteries
depend on general fund monies in addiƟ on to fees and perpetual care funds. The City of Cheyenne has an opportunity to
increase revenue’s signiﬁ cantly by providing tree sales, monuments, engraving and foundaƟ ons at the cemetery. This has
already begun to be implemented and will contribute to the overall revenue stream of the cemetery at the new locaƟ on as
well as subsidize operaƟ ons at the other city cemeteries. The City is currently charging a $150 base fee for engraving Ɵ les
for the memorial wall and monuments.
Table X.2 shows the esƟ mated actual expenditures and revenues for 2013 and the 2014 budget for the City of Cheyenne
and the three cemeteries surveyed within the outlying project area. The table shows that the City of Cheyenne covers
more of its expenditures with operaƟ ng revenues compared to Casper and Laramie at between 28 percent and 38 percent,
compared to 6 percent for Laramie and 2 percent for Casper. ScoƩ sbluﬀ ’s operaƟ ng revenues are higher, covering between
37 percent and 48 percent of expenditures depending on capital equipment expenditures.  The City of Cheyenne revenues
are likely to increase with the revenues generated from the monument and engraving business. All municipaliƟ es depend
on their Perpetual Care Funds and General Fund revenues. Laramie is highly dependent on General Fund monies and has
a policy of providing cemetery services to the populaƟ on based on tax revenues versus service fees.
SUMMARY OF RESULTS AND RECOMMENDATIONS FOR NEW CEMETERY OPERATIONS
In general, the City of Cheyenne cemetery operaƟ ons are eﬃ  ciently run and provide adequate services for the populaƟ on of
Cheyenne as compared to other cemeteries in Cheyenne and the project area. Overall cost of operaƟ ons for personnel, materials
and supplies is lower than cemeteries with fewer acres to maintain in some instances. It appears that the cemetery pricing, services,
and maintenance acƟ viƟ es are compeƟ Ɵ ve with other cemeteries in the area. It is diﬃ  cult to compare revenue generaƟ on since
some cemeteries surveyed support their operaƟ ons with a general fund allocaƟ on rather than trying to maximize revenues for fees
and services. Cheyenne does a good job in maintaining reasonable expenditures and compeƟ Ɵ ve pricing for revenue generaƟ on as
well as capitalizing on other revenue generaƟ ng opportuniƟ es.
It is anƟ cipated that the VA will exchange land for capital cost contribuƟ on associated with infrastructure at the new Cemetery
Arboretum. Currently the VA is in the process of compleƟ ng their Environmental Assessment (EA) for the project and coordinaƟ on
with the VA representaƟ ves will occur aŌ er the compleƟ on of the EA. It is a likely possibility that the City of Cheyenne will also be
able to contract maintenance services at the NaƟ onal Burial Grounds on the campus of the Cemetery Arboretum. These items will
be decided at a later Ɵ me in terms of land appraisal and maintenance contracƟ ng fees. Therefore, no analysis of capital ﬁ nancing
is included in this analysis.
Current burial fees at the cemeteries are in line with other cemeteries or in some instances slightly higher, however fees could be
increased based on more desirable locaƟ on within the cemetery. This is being done at the Mountain View Cemetery currently and
makes sense for the new Cemetery Arboretum. Costs should be aligned with compeƟ Ɵ ve pricing within Cheyenne parƟ cularly.
Adult burial space prices could be raised slightly at the new cemetery. Earth interments of single cremains sites should also be
available at the new cemetery.
Depending on burial space sales, the new cemetery may require one addiƟ onal full Ɵ me and one seasonal employee. If the city
maintains the VA NaƟ onal Burial Ground, 2 addiƟ onal full Ɵ me employees would be required plus seasonal employees. OperaƟ ons
at the new cemetery are likely to be more eﬃ  cient for mowing and irrigaƟ ng even though the area maintained will be greater. Cost
savings would occur due to: services taking place in the commiƩ al shelter with parking lot nearby, no lowering device required or
tent set-up, asphalt roads, kiosk showing spaces and plots, and a computerized database for genealogic records.
A green burial site is recommended as well as columbarium, memorial wall and angel sculpture. A green burial site requires water
table tests and should be located on the west side of the new cemetery site. The new cemetery site should also allow shrubs and
trees as well as upright monuments.
